    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eChuck roast is the cut this dish wants.\u003c/strong\u003e It needs a long braise to get tender, so the cheaper cut is also the correct one. Watch for the family pack price.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eOrzo is one of the cheapest pastas per pound.\u003c/strong\u003e It is also the only shape that works here, because it needs to cook in a shallow layer of liquid without clumping.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eCook the pasta in the sauce and save a pot.\u003c/strong\u003e That is not a shortcut, it is the actual traditional method, and it means all the flavor stays in the dish.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy parmesan as a wedge or tub.\u003c/strong\u003e This dish gets a serious amount of it stirred in and scattered on top, and the shaker can costs more per ounce for less cheese."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cut the beef chuck roast into large cubes and season them with salt and black pepper.",
                    "Heat the olive oil in a large oven-safe pot and brown the beef in batches, giving each piece room, then set it aside.",
                    "Cook the diced red onions and carrots in the same pot until softened and picking up the browned bits.",
                    "Stir in the tomato paste and cook it until it darkens, then add the diced tomatoes, ground cinnamon, sugar, and beef broth.",
                    "Return the beef, cover, and braise in a moderate oven until the meat is fork tender, which is the point of no return for this dish. Do not rush it.",
                    "Stir the dry orzo into the pot with enough hot water or broth to cover it, since the pasta will drink a surprising amount.",
                    "Return it to the oven uncovered until the orzo is tender and the liquid is mostly absorbed, then stir in most of the parmesan cheese and scatter the rest over the top."
                ],
    "portion_html":  "Weigh the whole pot, subtract the empty-pot weight from Step 1, and divide by 14. Stir it well before you weigh, because the orzo settles and the beef floats. Every serving comes in at \u003cstrong\u003e700 calories with 39 grams of protein\u003c/strong\u003e. Orzo keeps absorbing overnight, so a splash of water on reheat brings it right back.",
